# The diagnostics of the LEASE: a cell's name where it may not stand, a write
# to it that is not its own release, and a release of something that is no
# lease (design.md §4.19; M-held-bytes, panels 124 and 125).
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE. `lend_errors.hero` held the lend, the field lend
# and the lease as one family until panel 171's two refusals, `lend_kept` and
# `lend_needs_a_header`, put it past §11's 300 lines. The seam is the one the
# checker has carried since panel 124: `check/lending.hero` asks where a LEND
# may stand and `check/leasing.hero` where a LEASE may, and these three are that
# second module's alone. The three functions moved here verbatim, with the
# reasoning each carried.
#
# NO `Fix` HERE, for `lend_errors.hero`'s reason: every repair is a different
# shape of program, and a fix that leaves the defect standing is a guess.

use token
use diag

# A lease cell's name where it may not stand (M-held-bytes, panel 124 R2 to R4
# and panel 125's soundness lane).
#
# A lease is `x: cstr @ s.lease()`: a COPY of the bytes that the program owns
# and C may read until `end_lease(@x)` frees it. What makes a second release
# impossible is that the release nulls the CELL, so the pointer may live only
# in that one cell: a copy of it — `g = x`, `g: cstr @ x`, a field, a return —
# would go on pointing at the bytes after the cell was nulled, and reading it
# through C is a use-after-free `check` could not see. So the name of a lease
# cell may stand only as an argument of a call, exactly where a lend may.
function lease_escapes(name: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "lease_escapes",
        message: "`" + name + "` is a lease — a copy of the bytes that C may read until `end_lease(@" + name + ")` — and its name may stand only as an argument of a call; here it is copied, and the copy would outlive the release",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "pass `" + name + "` directly to the call that reads it. The release nulls this one cell, so a pointer that lives anywhere else keeps pointing at freed bytes"
    )

    # Defect 067's own half of the sentence above: a group's `record` is an
    # argument position that keeps, so `Box(p: x)` puts the pointer somewhere
    # the release cannot reach and `end_lease` leaves it dangling at exit 0.
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "building a `record` of an `extern` group is not such a call: the field keeps the pointer past `end_lease(@" + name + ")`, and C then reads freed bytes. Pass the cell to each call that reads it instead of storing it"
    )
    return d

# A write to a lease cell that is not its own `end_lease`.
function lease_written(name: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "lease_written",
        message: "`" + name + "` is a lease, and the only write a lease cell takes after its declaration is `end_lease(@" + name + ")` — a value stored over it would leak the bytes it held, or free bytes nobody leased",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "declare a second cell for the second lease. One cell, one `.lease()`, one `end_lease`"
    )
    return d

# `end_lease` on something that is not a lease cell.
function end_lease_of_no_lease(span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "end_lease_of_no_lease",
        message: "`end_lease` frees what `.lease()` made, and it takes the cell that holds it: `end_lease(@x)` where `x: cstr @ s.lease()` — this argument is not such a cell",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "a lend (`.cstr()`), a pointer from C and a literal are freed by whoever owns them, and this program does not; a lease copied into another cell cannot be released from there, because the release must null the one cell the pointer lives in"
    )
    return d
